  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 946 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nSatellite shows broad troughing over the Central US with ridging\nin place along the East and West Coasts. This trough has brought\na cooler Canadian air mass south into the Northern Plains which,\ncombined with cloud cover, is why temperatures were so cool \ntoday. Shower and storm chances have dropped south of our area\nalong with the cold front. A few showers may still clip Falls\nCity overnight tonight, but the rest of our area will remain\ndry. \n\nOvernight we'll start to see building gp-heights with the ridge\nto our west slowly expanding eastward. This combined with clear\nskies and calm winds over the northern 2/3rds of our area should\nsupport significant fog development. To the south, clouds have \nbeen slow to clear, but we could still see some fog develop over\nthese areas as well, especially if we see more clearing \novernight. Some of this fog across our area will likely be \ndense, and expect at some point we'll need a Dense Fog Advisory \nfor a large portion of our area.\n\nThursday starts out foggy but this should clear by 10am. The \nexpansion of the ridge eastward as well as increasing southerly \nflow will lead to warmer temperatures. High temperatures should\nreach into the low-to-mid 80s across the area, with some spots \nin northern Knox County approaching 90. \n\nWe'll remain on the east side of this stationary ridge going\ninto the weekend. With increased southerly flow ahead of a \ndeveloping area of low pressure on the lee-side of the Rockies, \nwe'll see temperatures climb into the upper 80s to low 90s over \nthe weekend. There is some uncertainty in high temperatures due\nto the GFS's tendency to over-mix, which causes a significant\nwarm bias in these patterns. The ECMWF and other long-range\nmodels are significantly cooler with highs staying int he upper\n80s to low 90s. Rain chances and lingering cloud cover could \ndampen daytime highs as well. Shortwaves riding down the \neastern side of the ridge will tap into the moisture advecting \nin from the Gulf leading to nocturnal storm chances starting \nFriday night through the weekend. Some of these storms could \nbecome severe, particularly on Friday and Saturday due to the \nstrengthening nocturnal LLJ and enhanced shear near a lingering \nfrontal boundary across our area. Periodic rain chances continue\ninto early next week with temperatures staying near normal.",
